What is a RESTful Web Service?

A RESTful Web Service is an application programming interface (API) that follows the REST (Representational State Transfer) architecture. It enables communication between client applications and servers using the HTTP protocol.

REST services are lightweight, easy to develop, and commonly exchange data in JSON format.

Features of REST
Uses HTTP methods like GET, POST, PUT, DELETE
Platform independent
Stateless communication
Supports JSON and XML data formats
Easy to integrate with web and mobile applications
Common HTTP Methods
Method	Purpose
GET	Retrieve data from server
POST	Insert new data
PUT	Update existing data
DELETE	Remove data
Spring Boot REST Controller

Spring Boot provides @RestController to create REST APIs.

What is @RestController?
Combines @Controller and @ResponseBody
Returns data directly instead of rendering JSP pages
Automatically converts Java objects into JSON
@GetMapping Annotation

@GetMapping maps an HTTP GET request to a Java method.

Example

@GetMapping("/hello")

Whenever a browser requests

http://localhost:8083/hello

the corresponding Java method executes.

1. Hello World REST API
HelloController.java
package com.cognizant.springlearn.controller;

import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.GetMapping;
import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.RestController;

@RestController
public class HelloController {

    @GetMapping("/hello")
    public String displayMessage() {

        System.out.println("Inside displayMessage()");

        String message = "Hello World!!";

        System.out.println("Returning response");

        return message;

    }

}
Explanation
@RestController

Marks the class as a REST Controller.

Spring automatically returns the method's result as an HTTP response.

@GetMapping("/hello")

Maps the URL

/hello

to the displayMessage() method.

Return Type
public String displayMessage()

Since the return type is String, the browser displays

Hello World!!
